; BT-9. THE TV SCREEN. A dying broadcast, ported from mp-foundation's TvPortal.cs.
;
; BT-10 sets this on the ported TV's screen quad IN PLACE OF the NoiseTexture2D StandardMaterial3D
; that TvPortal.cs builds in code. The flicker numbers are identical -- see the shader header:
; 1.05 + 0.25*sin(9.7t)*sin(2.3t), with a 1% stutter to 0.4 -- but the noise actually crawls here,
; and it costs no per-frame _Process callback and no per-TV material instance.
;
; ONE OVERRIDE, ADDED BY BT-9b (2026-08-28). Every OTHER default in `tv_static.gdshader` is still
; TvPortal.cs's value (albedo 0.75/0.78/0.80, emission 0.72/0.76/0.82, roughness 0.2) and the
; flicker curve is untouched. The override lives HERE rather than in the shader precisely so the
; shader's defaults stay the ported numbers and this file records, visibly, the one place where the
; port did not survive contact with this level's night. That is the same pattern `lake_murk.tres`
; uses and the opposite of the silent drift `water_surface.tres` warns about.
[gd_resource type="ShaderMaterial" load_steps=2 format=3]

[ext_resource type="Shader" path="res://resources/shaders/tv_static.gdshader" id="1_static"]

[resource]
render_priority = 0
shader = ExtResource("1_static")

; THE SCREEN MUST BE THE BRIGHTEST THING IN THE ROOM AT NIGHT (BT-10 measured it as a dark grey
; rectangle; packet BT-9b scope item 4, and emission numbers were explicitly handed to this role).
;
; WHY 1.2 COULD NOT WORK, AS ARITHMETIC RATHER THAN TASTE. The shader's emitted value is
; emission_tint * lum * emission_energy * pulse, where lum = noise * scanlines * vignette. The
; noise term is `mix(0.5, hash, 0.72)`, so its MEAN is 0.5, not 1.0 -- the screen spends most of
; its area at half brightness by construction, which is what makes it read as static rather than
; as a lamp. Scanlines take a further 0.94 and the vignette about 0.86. Mean lum is therefore
; ~0.404, and at energy 1.2 the mean emitted value is 0.390: a MID GREY. Nothing was broken; the
; ported number was simply chosen for a room that was not this one.
;
; 3.2 puts the mean emitted value at 1.041 and the centre peak at 2.743, i.e. the middle of the
; screen clips to white and blooms while the edges stay grey -- which is what a CRT in a dark room
; actually looks like. For scale, the brightest ORDINARY surface in the level at night is the new
; mid-grey neutral under `OutdoorAtmosphere.NightAmbientFloor` (0.05), at 0.027. The screen is
; ~38x that at its mean. It is unambiguously the brightest thing in the room.
;
; IT MUST NOT BECOME THE ROOM'S LIGHT SOURCE, and it does not: `tv_static.gdshader` writes EMISSION
; and carries no light node, so the screen is bright and the room stays dark. That separation is a
; directed constraint (/direct 2026-08-28), not an implementation convenience.
;
; THE FLICKER CURVE IS SCALED, NEVER RESHAPED. This is one multiplier on the whole expression, so
; the 9.7/2.3 Hz detuned pair and the 1% stutter to 0.4 keep their exact shape and relative depth.
; No new peak behaviour is introduced, which is what keeps the brownout a brownout and stops it
; becoming a strobe (THRILL-BIBLE 8.2 -- a startle is a sudden ADDITION, and there is no new one).
shader_parameter/emission_energy = 3.2
